AIM:
To solve the Tower of Hanoi problem using recursion and understand the working of recursive algorithms.

ALGORITHM:
Start with three rods: Source (A), Auxiliary (B), and Destination (C).
Let n be the number of disks.
If n == 1, move the disk directly from Source to Destination.
Otherwise:
Move n-1 disks from Source to Auxiliary using Destination as helper.
Move the nth disk from Source to Destination.
Move the n-1 disks from Auxiliary to Destination using Source as helper.
Repeat until all disks are moved to the Destination rod.

PROGRAM:
#include <stdio.h>
void towerOfHanoi(int n, char from_rod, char to_rod, char aux_rod) {
    if (n == 1) {
        printf("Move disk 1 from rod %c to rod %c\n", from_rod, to_rod);
        return;
    }
    towerOfHanoi(n - 1, from_rod, aux_rod, to_rod);
    printf("Move disk %d from rod %c to rod %c\n", n, from_rod, to_rod);
    towerOfHanoi(n - 1, aux_rod, to_rod, from_rod);
}
int main() {
    int n;
    printf("Enter number of disks: ");
    scanf("%d", &n);
    towerOfHanoi(n, 'A', 'C', 'B');
    return 0;
}

